00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:11,860
correct. So that means Mike is right about this professor being unfair.

255
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:15,220
Well, he could be, or it could be something else, like maybe a teacher's assistant

256
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:16,060
graded this paper.

257
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:20,040
When I was at TA, I graded undergrad papers, and I didn't know what I was doing.

258
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:22,140
I sure didn't.

259
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:26,960
Jason, I want to go down and give this Bozo professor a piece of my mind.

260
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:28,060
Maggie, come on.

261
00:12:28,060 --> 00:12:29,040
This is college.

262
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:32,580
How's Mike gonna feel if his mommy go down and talk to the professor?

263
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:35,540
He'd be embarrassed.

264
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:36,720
Embarrassed? Come on.

265
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:39,120
He's still bitter about the scene I made over the clay.

266
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:43,360
No, talking to the teacher would be absolutely the wrong thing to do.

267
00:12:46,520 --> 00:12:48,960
Excuse me. Hi, I'm Jason Seaver.

268
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:50,140
I'd like to talk to you about my son.

269
00:12:50,660 --> 00:12:55,080
Okay. Speaking colleague to colleague, I think it's important for him to be exposed,

270
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:57,920
at least, to the excitement and the wonder of our field.

271
00:12:58,380 --> 00:12:59,280
I could agree more.

272
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:02,720
Yeah, well, I'm just afraid he's gonna drop it without giving it a fair shot.

273
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:04,460
Oh, now, that'd be a shame.

274
00:13:04,780 --> 00:13:08,800
But colleague to colleague, if your kid don't want to be a janitor, why push him?

275
00:13:12,220 --> 00:13:13,840
Jim. Yo, Doc.

276
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:17,800
Oh, my God.

277
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:20,020
You, uh, Dr.

278
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:23,440
Rosenfeld? Yes. Yes, I'm, uh, I'm Jason Siever.

279
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:24,700
I'm Mike Siever's dad.

280
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:26,480
Mike Siever. Yes.

281
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,100
Mike Siever? Uh -huh, yeah, he's in your intro to psych class.

282
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:30,120
Curly hair, funny kid.

283
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:32,980
Well, I've got 300 students in that lecture class.

284
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:35,320
At least 100 of them have curly hair.

285
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:37,400
All of them think they're very funny.

286
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:38,660
Yeah, it's all bad.

287
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,520
Well, I just came to maybe clear up a little misunderstanding over his makeup

288
00:13:42,520 --> 00:13:46,320
assignment. Excuse me, but I always like to make a note when something bizarre

289
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:49,880
happens. Preposterous, ludicrous, queer.

290
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:52,120
Yes, I know what it means.

291
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:56,640
In all my years as a college professor, I've never had a parent -teacher conference.

292
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:58,420
Oh, well don't think of me as a parent, please.

293
00:13:58,420 --> 00:13:59,520
Oh, so you're not a parent.

294
00:13:59,620 --> 00:14:01,160
No, no, yes, I am a parent.

295
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:04,320
But I would like to talk to you, if I may, colleague to colleague.

296
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:07,440
Colleague? Equal, comrade, peer.

297
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:09,580
Associate, coadjutor,

298
00:14:10,560 --> 00:14:14,320
messmate. I could go on.

299
00:14:14,780 --> 00:14:16,400
In other words, I had a little book to write in.

300
00:14:17,540 --> 00:14:18,620
I am a psychiatrist.

301
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:22,020
Oh, well, why didn't you say so?

302
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:23,740
Please, sit down, sit down.

303
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:24,620
This is wonderful.

304
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:26,360
Are you an educational psych?

305
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:27,600
No, private practice.

306
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:30,080
Oh, that's great.

307
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:31,940
I almost went that route myself.

308
00:14:32,360 --> 00:14:35,120
Well, just invest in a leather couch here and you're halfway there.

309
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:39,400
Pipe? No, I don't smoke, thank you.

310
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:40,360
Neither do I.

311
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:47,240
So, Dr. Siever, what's on your mind?

312
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:52,220
Well, it's this darn makeup assignment in psych.

313
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:56,000
It seems that you or more likely one of your teacher's assistants may have misgraded

314
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,980
it. Miscrated? Yeah, like right there, that one.

315
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:04,500
I don't use TAs and I don't misgrade papers.

316
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:07,240
If there's a crimson check mark next to it, it's wrong.

317
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:09,180
Oh, no, believe me, this one's not wrong.

318
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:10,540
No, no, I checked this one carefully.

319
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:12,200
So did I.

320
00:15:13,380 --> 00:15:16,120
Okay, well, let me show you here, for example.

321
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:22,400
Look, A, Freudian analysis, B, Jungian analysis, C, transactional analysis, D, A and

322
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:28,080
B but not C, E, A and C but not B, F, B and C but not A, G, all of the above.

323
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:30,680
E is clear to the right answer, and yet, marked wrong.

324
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:34,920
It's not as much wrong as it is imprecise.

325
00:15:35,380 --> 00:15:37,540
Imprecise? Ambiguous, unclear.

326
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:38,440
Yes, I know what it means.

327
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:39,600
Yes, how was that in precise?

328
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:42,860
Well, the correct answer is D, A and B, but not C.

329
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:46,560
Of course, if the study in Paris goes the other way, I'll consider changing the

330
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:49,340
answer, but we'll have to wait until spring of 94.

331
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:53,900
Uh -huh. Okay, look at number five.

332
00:15:54,020 --> 00:15:55,900
The correct answer is checked, B.

333
00:15:56,700 --> 00:16:00,400
Hyperkinesis is a physiological and psychological disorder.

334
00:16:00,620 --> 00:16:01,720
It's marked wrong.

335
00:16:01,840 --> 00:16:03,120
Sure, because it is wrong.

336
00:16:03,180 --> 00:16:04,960
Since when? Since Dr.

337
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:07,180
Kirchenbauer's recent project with white rats.

338
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:11,400
Pardon me? I'm sorry, I can see where your point of view might be considered correct

339
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:12,500
in some circles.

340
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:13,900
Okay, then so we're both right.

341
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:17,740
Yes, but I'm teaching the class, which makes me more right.

342
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:21,100
What? Dr. Siever, how can I say this?

343
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:25,720
I suggest that you're looking at your son's work through a father's eyes, and not

344
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:29,820
through the eyes of a competent professional, which of course I'm sure you are in

345
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:31,100
your own little world.

346
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:34,640
I'm not looking at my son's work that way.

347
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:35,280
Yes you are.

348
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:35,780
No, I'm not.

349
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:36,320
Yes you are.

350
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:37,520
I'm not. R, R, R.

351
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:38,820
No, no, no, no, look.

352
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:42,780
This is not my son's work, Bob.

353
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:47,240
Pal, messmate. I wrote these answers myself.

354
00:16:47,460 --> 00:16:50,120
Well, then I definitely should reevaluate this grade.

355
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:52,040
Thank you very much, I should hope so.

356
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:56,160
You're using a red pen.

357
00:16:56,840 --> 00:17:07,500
No, crimson. Just a minute, I'm coming.

358
00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:10,860
Flowers for Julie Costello.

359
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:12,300
Who are they from?

360
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:13,960
Your secret boyfriend.

361
00:17:14,360 --> 00:17:16,760
Maurice? No, the other one.

362
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:21,760
Something's wrong.

363
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:26,120
No. Where are your books?

364
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:27,960
I thought we were studying for psych tonight.

365
00:17:27,960 --> 00:17:33,220
How would you like to stand on the windblown deck of the Staten Island Ferry and

366
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:36,600
watch the sun go down and the lights of Manhattan come up?

367
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:38,380
Hmm, would you be with me?

368
00:17:38,720 --> 00:17:40,380
Of course I'd be with you.

369
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:41,740
Then I don't want to go.

370
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:46,820
Mike, you can't keep ignoring your problems in psych class.

371
00:17:46,880 --> 00:17:48,720
I'm not. I'm dropping the class.

372
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:50,220
Why would you want to drop it?

373
00:17:50,220 --> 00:17:53,780
Listen, listen, before you say another word, remember how I asked you to constantly

374
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:54,620
remind me to study?

375
00:17:54,860 --> 00:17:57,360
Yeah. Well, I no longer want you to do that anymore.

376
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:00,820
Why? Because you constantly remind me to study.

377
00:18:01,640 --> 00:18:04,620
But you... Hey, listen, Julie, have me at my mind about this class thing, okay?

378
00:18:05,100 --> 00:18:09,100
Now, can't we have a nice romantic evening involving two burros of the city of New

379
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:12,500
York? Okay. I'll get my coat.

380
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:15,360
All right. I even made dinner reservations.

381
00:18:16,300 --> 00:18:17,520
Well, this'll be fun.

382
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:20,940
And it's good that we're spending time together now.

383
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:22,340
What do you mean, now?

384
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:26,180
Well, you'll have to make up this class, and that pretty much takes care of our

385
00:18:26,180 --> 00:18:30,760
summer plans and they were great plans.

386
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:34,820
Well, maybe I was being a little bit mean.

387
00:18:35,860 --> 00:18:36,960
You think so?

388
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:38,020
Oh, yeah, yeah.

389
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:41,500
I mean, hey, I'm halfway through the semester already, and so if I can just hang in

390
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:43,020
there a couple more weeks...

391
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:44,640
Well, if that's what you want to do.

392
00:18:44,680 --> 00:18:45,840
Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.

393
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:46,720
I should study.

394
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:49,120
But I left my books at home.

395
00:18:49,740 --> 00:18:51,600
So, look, we're gonna have to go out another night, okay?

396
00:18:52,300 --> 00:18:55,600
I was looking so forward to going out tonight.

397
00:18:55,600 --> 00:18:57,940
Yeah. See ya.

398
00:18:59,340 --> 00:19:01,880
Hey, at least I won't have to review the chapter on manipulation.

399
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:07,800
Hey, Mom. Hey, Mike.

400
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,160
I thought you were eating out.

401
00:19:09,300 --> 00:19:10,260
Yes, so did I.

402
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:11,060
What's for dinner?

403
00:19:11,220 --> 00:19:12,160
That's what I'd like to know.

404
00:19:12,380 --> 00:19:13,120
Have you seen your father?

405
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:14,720
Many, many times.

406
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:18,120
He didn't make anything for dinner.

407
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:21,280
Hey, Mom, between the two of us, I think the man slipped a cog.

408
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:22,860
No, no, no, honey.

409
00:19:22,860 --> 00:19:24,660
He has always been that way.

410
00:19:25,460 --> 00:19:28,640
Hello. I brought a hummus for everyone.

411
00:19:30,180 --> 00:19:31,380
You might be on to something.

412
00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:33,980
Okay, why beat around the bush?

413
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:37,380
Bush? Shrugged small tree, President of the United States.

414
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:39,980
You're definitely on to something.

415
00:19:40,520 --> 00:19:43,840
I'll be the first to concede that things didn't go exactly as I'd planned.

416
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,020
What things? Okay, I'll tell you.

417
00:19:46,780 --> 00:19:49,240
But keep in mind, there is some good news...

418
00:19:50,020 --> 00:19:50,680
all the way at the end.

419
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:53,740
Why don't we go right to the good news?

420
00:19:53,740 --> 00:19:55,560
The good news is, I believe in Mike.

421
00:19:56,500 --> 00:19:59,300
Thank you. And the other news?

422
00:19:59,660 --> 00:20:02,000
I met your psychology professor today.

423
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:06,040
What? You went down there after telling me not to?

424
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:08,420
Dan, that is the worst thing you could have possibly done.

425
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:09,980
That's exactly what I told your mother.

426
00:20:10,120 --> 00:20:11,320
I can't believe this.

427
00:20:11,520 --> 00:20:13,740
My daddy goes to see my college professor?

428
00:20:14,260 --> 00:20:16,300
How am I supposed to show my face there again?

429
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:17,400
Mike, come on.

430
00:20:17,400 --> 00:20:18,980
I didn't go down there as your father.

431
00:20:19,140 --> 00:20:20,600
I went down as a colleague.

432
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:23,940
All right, I see you're having trouble making the distinction here, right?

433
00:20:24,940 --> 00:20:26,520
He didn't bring him home us, did you?

434
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:31,960
No. Well, good, because I gotta go face that guy in the morning.

435
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:33,440
I thought you gave up that class.

436
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:34,680
I changed my mind.

437
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:35,540
What did you do that for?

438
00:20:35,680 --> 00:20:36,880
Mike, you were right about that guy.

439
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:37,460
He's a bozo.

440
00:20:37,660 --> 00:20:39,120
You're never gonna get a fair shake.

441
00:20:39,360 --> 00:20:40,620
Jason, what are you doing?

442
00:20:40,700 --> 00:20:42,400
Trust me. Not a chance.

443
00:20:43,240 --> 00:20:45,160
I was halfway through the semester.

444
00:20:45,460 --> 00:20:48,840
I mean, I just have to make up that D that I got on the makeup assignment.

445
00:20:49,340 --> 00:20:53,080
Yeah, well, I told him that all the answers on that test were correct.

446
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:58,040
And so, and you don't exactly have a D on it anymore.

447
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:00,040
Uh -oh, what do you mean?

448
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:04,020
You mean you got him to change my grade?

449
00:21:04,660 --> 00:21:05,860
All right, dad.

450
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:09,060
Aw, you can embarrass me any day.

451
00:21:09,900 --> 00:21:11,280
Okay, I'll be frank.

452
00:21:12,460 --> 00:21:19,300
I, and I also, when I was there I mentioned, I said to him, I said, and I myself

453
00:21:19,300 --> 00:21:21,220
wrote the answers.

454
00:21:21,220 --> 00:21:23,960
and so, uh, you change your grade and you have an F.

455
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:30,300
What? What? It wasn't part of the plan, Maggie.

456
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:32,300
Oh, Jason, you shouldn't have even had a plan.

457
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:34,260
Oh, so now I gotta go and work off an F?

458
00:21:34,820 --> 00:21:36,860
Oh, what, like you've never had an F before?

459
00:21:39,840 --> 00:21:41,180
Jason! I'm sorry!

460
00:21:41,380 --> 00:21:43,720
I'm upset. I've never had an F before.

461
00:21:44,240 --> 00:21:48,140
And may I remind you, this is the proper place for that good news I mentioned

462
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:50,640
earlier. What good news?

463
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:52,140
That I believe in, Mike.

464
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:59,380
Yeah! Dad, it'll be another 12 years before I ever show you my homework.

465
00:22:00,220 --> 00:22:02,760
Oh, well, with that attitude, you'll still be in the same grade.

466
00:22:05,700 --> 00:22:09,520
Oh! Do you believe this guy?

467
00:22:10,340 --> 00:22:13,800
I mean, of all the things that he could've learned out of this, that there are no

468
00:22:13,800 --> 00:22:17,980
bad answers in life, that occasionally people are gonna treat you unfairly, that

469
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:21,100
sometimes a father maybe gets a little nutty when he's looking out for his kid.

470
00:22:21,220 --> 00:22:22,380
But no, what does he take home?

471
00:22:22,620 --> 00:22:23,760
Don't show me his homework.

472
00:22:24,580 --> 00:22:33,040
Oh boy. Honey?

473
00:22:35,300 --> 00:22:36,400
Works for me.

474
00:22:42,300 --> 00:22:44,000
Hey Mike, I bet you $5.

475
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:46,620
Carol can do this next problem in two seconds.

476
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:48,040
All right, you're on.

477
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:50,220
Carol? A chance to get Mike?

478
00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:51,880
I love it.

479
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:58,700
Okay. Ha! X equals the square root of 12.

480
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:00,720
That'll be five bucks, please.

481
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:02,840
All right, Double Nothing says she can't do the next one.

482
00:23:03,100 --> 00:23:05,340
You're on. My pleasure.

483
00:23:06,340 --> 00:23:08,300
Y equals 6.7.

484
00:23:08,460 --> 00:23:09,760
All right, that's 10 bucks.

485
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:12,280
Okay, Double Nothing says she can't finish the whole page.

486
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:14,200
You don't stand a chance.

487
00:23:14,540 --> 00:23:17,200
Mike, get ready to lose your behind.

488
00:23:21,480 --> 00:23:24,740
LAUGHTER APPLAUSE
